Code for running AMOS simulations of Ragazzoni/Tokovonin least squares tomography algorithm for my AMOS 2023 poster.

Process is as follows:

basis functions in use here are:
- Zernike polynomials
- DCT
- Wavelets
- ridgelets

for each set of basis functions:
    for each layer:
        generate phase screen with the same pixel spacial density
    for each guide star:
        cut out telescope projections from phase screen
    for target:
        cut out telescope projection from phase screen
    START TIMING HERE
    for each guide star cut-out:
        calculate basis function coefficients using Ideal Wavefront Sensing
    run tomography on guide star basis function coefficients to estimate target wavefront coefficients 
    reproduce target wavefront using calculated coefficients
    END TIMING HERE
    calculate MSSIM and MSE of reproduction compared to original wavefront

For the autoencoder, the process is similar, but uses an autoencoder instead of Ideal Wavefront Sensing for the basis function calculation.
The latent space of the autoencoder is used as the input to the tomographic algorithm, then the autoencoder decoding step is used to reproduce the target wavefront.

Ideal Wavefront Sensing uses the 2D dot product of the wavefront with the basis functions to calculate the coefficients for reproduction.

Noise can be added by adding gaussian noise directly to the calculated coefficients, or by adding noise to the wavefront representation before calculating the coefficents.

Phase screens are a single set of randomised Kolmogorov turbulence. Each test will use the same set of Kolmogorov phase screens.

Asterisms are sets of ideal laser guide stars in symmetric patterns, ideal natural guide stars in random patters, and sets of natural guide stars with differences in apparent magnitude

Target locations are static with on-axis compared to off-axis in X, Y, 45deg and other confgurations.

Dynamic simulations will be used where the target locations and phase screen are moved independently, to simulate a spatio-temporally variant atmoshper and satellite position
Dynamic movements will simulate different angular velocities of the satellite across some chord of the telescope aperture projection. This may or may not pass through the optical axis.
Phase screens will move in some direction, and at some wind speed. Each layer will have different wind directions and speeds.

